{"type":"user","uuid":"01a0a97c-2e54-76d8-a43f-eda9f49a7def","session_id":"01a0a97b-e835-7c2d-ae4b-868760aef091","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_01a0a97c0c4a7047bd729ee7","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"Model Context Protocol MCP Anthropic definition\",\n  \"totalResults\": 13,\n  \"showing\": 10,\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/\",\n      \"title\": \"What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)? - Model Context Protocol\",\n      \"snippet\": \"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems. Using MCP, AI applications like Claude or ChatGPT can connect to data sources (e.g. local files, databases), tools (e.g. search engines, calculators) and workflows (e.g. specialized prompts)—enabling them to access key information and perform tasks. Think of MCP like a USB-C port for AI applications. Just as USB-C provides a standardized way to connect electronic devices, MCP provides a [.\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.8106142,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/getting-started/intro\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?\\n\\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems.\\n\\nUsing MCP, AI applications like Claude or ChatGPT can connect to data sources (e.g. local files, databases), tools (e.g. search engines, calculators) and wor\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Overview\\n\\nThe Model Context Protocol consists of several key components that work together:\\n\\n- Base Protocol: Core JSON-RPC message types\\n- Versioning and Compatibility: Protocol version negotiation, extension negotiation, and interoperability with earlier protocol revisions\\n- Message Patterns: Messaging pat\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/architecture/index\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Architecture\\n\\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) follows a client-host-server architecture where each\\nhost can run multiple client instances. MCP is a stateless protocol: every request is\\nself-contained and carries its own protocol version and capabilities.\\nThis architecture enables users to integrate AI capab\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026%2D07%2D28\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Specification\\n\\nModel Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that\\nenables seamless integration between LLM applications and external data sources and\\ntools. Whether you're building an AI-powered IDE, enhancing a chat interface, or creating\\ncustom AI workflows, MCP provides a standardized way to connect LL\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/server-concepts\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Understanding MCP servers\\n\\nMCP servers are programs that expose specific capabilities to AI applications through standardized protocol interfaces.\\n\\nCommon examples include file system servers for document access, database servers for data queries, GitHub servers for code management, Slack servers for team co\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/tags/mcp\",\n      \"title\": \"Mcp | Model Context Protocol Blog\",\n      \"snippet\": \"open standard for providing affordances for developers to include UI components in their MCP clients. ... [...] The Enterprise-Managed Authorization extension is now stable. Organizations can centrally manage authorization for MCP servers and end-users can access all connected MCP servers through a single log in. The extension is being adopted by Anthropic, Microsoft, Okta and a growing number of MCP servers. The Enterprise-Managed Authorization (EMA) extension is now stable. We’ve heard from th\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.5440511,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/client-concepts\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Understanding MCP clients\\n\\nMCP clients are instantiated by host applications to communicate with particular MCP servers. The host application, like Claude.ai or an IDE, manages the overall user experience and coordinates multiple clients. Each client handles one direct communication with one server.\\n\\nUnderst\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026%2D07%2D28/learn/architecture\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"snippet\": \"> ## Documentation Index\\n> \\n> Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/llms.txt\\n> Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\\n\\n# Architecture overview\\n\\nThis overview of the Model Context Protocol (MCP) discusses its scope and core concepts, and provides an example demonstrating each core concept.\\n\\nBecause MCP SDKs abstract away many concerns, most developers will likely find the data layer protocol section to be the most useful. It di\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-11-21-mcp-apps\",\n      \"title\": \"MCP Apps: Extending servers with interactive user interfaces | Model Context Protocol Blog\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Anthropic, together with the MCP-UI creators and lead maintainers of the MCP UI Community Working Group. The MCP-UI project, created by Ido Salomon and Liad Yosef and maintained by a dedicated community, spearheaded the vision of agentic apps with interactive interfaces. To ensure interoperability and establish consistent security and usage patterns across the ecosystem, Anthropic, OpenAI, and MCP-UI are collaborating to create an official MCP extension for interactive interfaces. We’re [...] Th\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.4526091,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0356df675b434a839bc5b2507d328c75\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [],\n  \"answersAvailable\": true,\n  \"providerAnsw